fix(core): FIX-133 Recorder catches Throwable, so a crashed step leaves a row

`Recorder.wrap` caught `Exception`, so a step that threw an `Error` propagated
with no row at all and the message's timeline showed it as a step that never ran.
For a platform whose product is monitoring, a failure the timeline cannot show is
a product defect. One line now catches `Throwable`, writes the FAILED row with the
throwable's text and rethrows it unchanged by precise rethrow, which compiled with
no `throws` clause as D-111 expected. No control flow moved: same `!handedOn[0]`
and `policy.records` guards, same `record` call.

The form is `Throwable`, not the `RuntimeException | Error` that D-111 first
prescribed by copying D-104. That would have been a NARROWING here, because the
old `catch (Exception)` deliberately caught the checked `InterruptedException`
that D-056's stop-deadline cancel throws sneakily; it would have deleted the
FAILED row of every cancelled delivery to gain one for an `Error`. The mutant log
shows it: with `RuntimeException | Error`, `aCancelledStepRecordsItsFailureSnapshot`
fails with `[ingress, a]`. `Exception | Error` would close that hole but still
miss a direct `Throwable` subclass, and the mutant for it was killed by the one
case written for that distinction. D-111 was amended before any code was written.

Case 3, `anErrorAfterTheStepHandedOnLeavesNoSecondRow`, was written three times
and is the part of this commit worth reading. As the packet specified it, it was
green against the unfixed code, because today's narrow catch hid the very guard it
targets. The test-author added a witness so the method could fail for a stated
reason. The implementer then found the witness was carrying the whole method: the
guard half could not fail under ANY mutant in `Recorder`, because
`InMemoryMessageStore:39` keeps only the first row per `(id, step)`, so the second
FAILED row that a dropped `!handedOn[0]` writes never reached `timeline()`. A test
named "leaves no second row" that could not observe a second row. It now counts
the rows handed to the store, through a `CountingStore` wrapping the store
interface — the allowed boundary, not a mock of the class under test — and the
mutant dies at `RecorderTest:388` with `<[OK, FAILED]>`.

That is a class of vacuity rather than one bug, and it is the same drop F-29 is
about: any assertion that a second row is ABSENT for one `(id, step)` is
unfalsifiable, whatever the production code does. agent/NOTES.md records the
question to ask before asserting an absence — which layer swallows it?

A test the implementer did not write was changed, which CLAUDE.md allows only to
fix a wrong expectation with the reason here. `RedeliveredTimelineTest` case 2 was
renamed from `...WithNoFailureAnywhere` to
`aMessageWhoseFirstRunCrashedShowsTheCrashAsAFailedRowStitchedBeforeTheRedeliveredRun`,
and seven expectations flipped: run 1's interim timeline gains `3 check FAILED`,
`recorded("check")` goes 0 -> 1, "check" moves into the two-row loop, the final
step-3 row becomes FAILED, "no row is FAILED" becomes "step 3 is the only FAILED
row", step 3's `x-run` goes 2 -> 1 and its ATTEMPT goes `of(2)` -> `empty()`. Those
expectations recorded the defect, not intended behaviour: run 1's FAILED row now
wins the `putIfAbsent` over run 2's OK row. Beyond the seven flips, one assertion
was ADDED at line 230 — that the error text names "crashed at check on the first
run" — which the reviewer asked be named here rather than counted among them.

Evidence, all observed. Red first: each new case failed with `[ingress, a]` for
`[ingress, a, x]`. Green: `RecorderTest` 14/0/0/0. Final tree after the case-3
rewrite: `-pl flottila-core -am clean verify` exit 0, BUILD SUCCESS, core
511/0/0/0; `ci/coverage_check.py` exit 0, "coverage: no decrease", core 99.6 line
/ 99.5 branch, api 99.3 / 100.0. That run carries NO `-Pit`, so it is not a full
reactor run and this commit claims none: the earlier full `-Pit` run predates the
rewrite and does not describe this tree. Six mutants killed, each restored with
`cp` plus `touch` and proven byte-identical with `cmp`: the catch narrowed to
`Exception`, to `RuntimeException | Error`, to `Error`, to `Exception | Error`;
`!handedOn[0]` dropped; `throw failure` removed (compile error).

Not killable, and deliberately so: the `policy.records(..., FAILED)` guard. For a
FAILED outcome `records` is true under ALL, ERRORS and ENDS alike, and the only
false level, OFF, returns the bare stage at `:71` before the catch exists, so the
guard is unreachable-false. JaCoCo corroborates it independently — the only missed
branch in `Recorder` is that one, "1 of 4 branches missed". It stays in the code
because it states the policy correctly and goes live the moment a level declines
FAILED; removing it would be a trap, not a cleanup. Proof in agent/NOTES.md so no
later review spends a round on it.

Raised by this review and in these ledgers: FIX-140, because `record` runs BEFORE
the rethrow and can itself throw — `Trail.add` does `flush().await()` at
`Trail.java:90`, which an interrupt turns into a sneaky `InterruptedException` — so
the original throwable can be replaced by the recording's own failure. For an
`Error` that matters more than it sounds: the replacement is an `Exception`, and
`Parking.java:49` would park it, making transient what D-098 and D-111 both say is
not. D-111 is qualified accordingly: "rethrown unchanged" holds only when
recording succeeds. `Parking.java:49` itself is unchanged and must stay so.

docs/findings/F-29.md now closes the half this fixes and states what it does not:
the timeline is still stitched from two runs, the seam is still marked only by
`Envelope.ATTEMPT` in run 2's props, and surviving rows are still chosen by which
run wrote first. The dangerous shape is gone — a crashed run no longer reads as one
clean successful run — but a run-scoped key remains CONSIDER-12's.

